      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "When to Choose SGLT2 Inhibitors First"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Heart Failure: If the patient has heart failure (especially with reduced ejection fraction), SGLT2 inhibitors are strongly preferred due to proven reductions in heart failure hospitalizations.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD): If the patient has established CKD (down to low eGFR thresholds), SGLT2 inhibitors provide the strongest evidence for slowing kidney function decline.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Preference for Pills: Patients who prefer taking an oral daily pill over self-injecting a medication.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2,
              5
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "When to Choose GLP-1 Receptor Agonists First"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Higher A1c or Weight-Loss Goals: GLP-1s provide more potent blood sugar (A1c) lowering and significantly greater weight loss compared to SGLT2 inhibitors.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Stroke Prevention & High ASCVD Risk: If the primary vascular threat is atherosclerotic disease or a high risk of stroke, GLP-1s show an advantage.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Convenience of Dosing: Many modern GLP-1s are taken just once a week via sub-cutaneous pen injection.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Can You Use Both?"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Yes. Guidelines and clinical evidence show that combining an SGLT2 inhibitor and a GLP-1 receptor agonist offers complementary, additive benefits for heart, kidney, and metabolic control if a single agent is insufficient (though insurance coverage and cost can be a barrier).",
        "reference_indexes": [
          7,
          8
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "To help narrow down which option fits best, could you share:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Does the patient have a history of heart failure, kidney disease, or stroke?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Is the preference for an oral pill or an injectable medication?"
          }
        ]
      }
